                                            <div class="about">
                                                <h2 class="about-animal">About</h2>
                                                <div class="text-animal">
                                                    <div class="OutlineElement Ltr SCXW171254536 BCX0">
                                                        <p class="Paragraph SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                               Mandrills are the largest monkeys in the world, 
                                                               characterised by a red stripe down their elongated 
                                                               muzzles with blue ridges on the sides. They may look like 
                                                               baboons, but they are more closely related to mangabeys.
                                                            </span>
                                                        </p>                          

                                                        <p class="Paragraph SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8"></span>
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                                Mandrills use their teeth to express a variety of emotions 
                                                                and behaviours. Their sharp canines are used for defence, but 
                                                                a quick flash of their teeth is a friendly gesture, and a yawn 
                                                                is a warning not to approach!
                                                            </span>
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="Paragraph SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8"></span>
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                                These primates are incredibly social, living in large groups called hordes, 
                                                                with a hierarchy of dominant males and females at the top. The dominant males 
                                                                ultimately lead and father most of the offspring. They are sexually dimorphic, 
                                                                with males being about a third heavier than females.
                                                            </span>
                                                        </p>

                                                        <p class="Paragraph SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8"></span>
                                                            <span class="NormalTextRun SCXW5247660 BCX8">
                                                                Mandrill hordes live in groups of up to 50 individuals, spending 
                                                                their days on the ground and sleeping in the trees at night. Females 
                                                                raise the young and provide most of the care to the offspring.
                                                            </span>
                                                        </p>

